initions only mutates its arguments (all locally created), there's no state pollution. I might add a brief comment noting tests are self-contained. \n\nWait, one more consideration on `Reference(to=\"Pet\")`: in the real typesystem, Reference with a string `to` and definitions=None \u2014 creating it is fine. But `Reference.__init__` in this environment: `self.definitions = definitions` \u2014 fine.\n\nAlso `Reference(to=\"Pet\", definitions=existing)` \u2014 fine.\n\nOne risk: `Field()` bare instantiation \u2014 does base Field require anything? In typesystem, `Field(**kwargs)` with all optional. Fine.\n\n`Array()` \u2014 items=None default. Fine.\n\n`Object()` \u2014 properties=None \u2192 {}. Fine.\n\nNow, the try/except import: if `typesystem.schemas` has no `Reference`, ImportError is raised by the from-import; fallback to fields. If fields also lacks it... then the environment is broken; can't help. \n\nHmm, actually \u2014 wait. What if `typesystem.schemas.Reference` exists but is NOT a class compatible with what I construct... no, it's the Reference class.\n\nAnother thought: should the fallback order be fields-first? Consider: if schemas.py defines its own Reference AND fields.py has a different Reference (bizarre), using schemas' is correct for isinstance. If schemas imports from fields, same object. So schemas-first is strictly better. Keep it.\n\nLet me also double-check the `set_definitions` return: it returns None implicitly. `assert result is None` \u2014 fine.\n\nOne more coverage consideration: line 37's `field.definitions is None` short-circuit \u2014 when field is NOT a Reference, `field.definitions` is never evaluated (short-circuit). Covered by array/object/plain tests.\n\nLine 39 elif reached when field is not Reference: array tests. Line 46 elif reached when field is neither Reference nor Array: object tests and plain field test.\n\nI think the test file is complete. Let me also consider adding `@pytest.mark.parametrize`? Not needed.\n\nDo I n