ject *cls, PyObject *args, PyObject *kwds)\n{\n    Py_ssize_t nargs = PyTuple_GET_SIZE(args);\n    assert(nargs == 1 || nargs == 3);\n\n    /* Call object.__init__(self) now. */\n    /* XXX Could call super(type, cls).__init__() but what's the point? */\n    return object_init(cls, NULL, NULL);\n}\n```\n\nStill ignores kwds. OK so it works.\n\nBut actually, wait \u2014 typesystem itself uses this pattern, and typesystem is a real, tested library. Let me check the actual typesystem tests to see how they test definitions. From typesystem's test_schemas.py:\n\n```python\ndef test_schema_definitions():\n    definitions = SchemaDefinitions()\n\n    class Example(Schema, definitions=definitions):\n        name = String()\n\n    assert definitions[\"Example\"] == Example\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly, but something like that. Actually, let me look at real typesystem source. typesystem/schemas.py (from the repo):\n\n```python\nimport typing\nfrom abc import ABCMeta\nfrom typesystem.fields import Field\n\nclass SchemaDefinitions(dict):\n    def __getitem__(self, key: str) -> typing.Union[Field, type]:\n        try:\n            return super().__getitem__(key)\n        except KeyError:\n            message = f\"No definition for {key!r} found.\"\n            raise KeyError(message) from None\n\n    def __setitem__(self, key: str, value: typing.Union[Field, type]) -> None:\n        assert (\n            key not in self\n        ), f'Definition for {key!r} is already defined in the definitions.'\n        super().__setitem__(key, value)\n\n\nclass SchemaMetaclass(ABCMeta):\n    def __new__(\n        cls: type,\n        name: str,\n        bases: typing.Sequence[type],\n        attrs: dict,\n        definitions: typing.Optional[SchemaDefinitions] = None,\n    ) -> type:\n        fields: typing.Dict[str, Field] = {}\n\n        for key, value in list(attrs.items()):\n            if isinstance(value, Field):\n                attrs.pop(key)\n                fields[key] = value\n\n        # If this class is subclassing other Schema classes, add their fields.\n        for base in reversed(bases):\n            base_fields = getattr(base, \"fields\", {})\n            for key, value in base_fields.items():\n                if isinstance(value, Field) and key not in fields:\n                    fields[key] = value\n\n        # Add the definitions to any `Reference` fields that we're including.\n        if definitions is not None:\n            for field in fields.values():\n                set_definitions(field, definitions)\n\n        #  Sort fields by their actual position in the source code,\n        # using `Field._creation_counter`\n        attrs[\"fields\"] = dict(\n            sorted(fields.items(), key=lambda item: item[1]._creation_counter)\n        )\n\n        new_type = super(SchemaMetaclass, cls).__new__(  # type: ignore\n            cls, name, bases, attrs\n        )\n\n        if definitions is not None:\n            definitions[name] = new_type\n\n        return new_type\n\n\nclass Schema(metaclass=SchemaMetaclass):\n    fields: typing.Dict[str, Field] = {}\n\n    def __init__(self, *args: typing.Any, **kwargs: typing.Any) -> None:\n        if kwargs:\n            validate(kwargs, self)\n        for key, value in kwargs.items():\n            setattr(self, key, value)\n\n    def __eq__(self, other: typing.Any) -> bool:\n        ...\n```\n\nAnd `set_definitions`:\n\n```python\ndef set_definitions(field: Field, definitions: \"SchemaDefinitions\") -> None:\n    if isinstance(field, Reference):\n        assert field.definitions is None, ...\n        field.definitions = definitions\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually let me recall:\n\n```python\ndef set_definitions(field: Field, definitions: SchemaDefinitions) -> None:\n    if isinstance(field, Reference):\n        field.definitions = definitions\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not sure of the exact implementation. Let me use get_info to check. Actually the get_info tool presumably has access to the pro